  "notes": "The third A Touch of Jazz keyboardist-producer, included because \"the Jill Scott / Musiq sound cannot be modelled from Carvin & Ivan and Dre & Vidal alone\". `confidence` is **low** — the research rates the entry medium-low, sourcing his role inside the Philadelphia production house but marking the parametric detail as lane inference. The entry is explicit that drums are not his role: \"the drum layer on his co-productions is the house programmed-plus-live-percussion grid, backbeat on 2&4, swung 16th hats\", so the kit is authored as the house grammar at its own numbers rather than copied from either of the other two Philadelphia models — a lower kick density, a smaller ghost map and a three-lane percussion set. His function is stated as harmonic: \"acoustic-piano and Rhodes beds with 7th/9th/11th voicings, gospel passing chords, and real 8-bar motion. **Model him as the chord author on tracks where the drums come from someone else.**\" That is the ninth-weighted extension spread, `susOrDimProb` for the passing chords, chord durations of 2–8 beats and a harmonic rhythm that moves every bar. The one thing that separates him from his colleagues is named: \"acoustic piano as the lead timbre (more than Rhodes, which distinguishes him inside the Philadelphia group)\", and \"melodic countermelody comes from piano fills answering the vocal in the mid-register\" — which is the piano timbre hint and a −12 counter at a 0.6 density ratio. Bass is \"played bass; no 808\". ⚠ Every drum number and every melody figure here is INFERRED.",
